DATA = [{'filename': 'como-conectar-los-espacios-interiores-y-exteriores.html', 'title': 'How to Connect Indoor and Outdoor Spaces at Home', 'desc': 'Learn how to create a more harmonious transition between indoor rooms and a patio, terrace, balcony, or garden using coordinated colors, materials, plants, furniture, and lighting.', 'intro': 'Connecting indoor and outdoor spaces can make a home feel more coherent and inviting. Repeating selected colors, materials, textures, plants, and visual details can create a natural transition without making both areas look identical.', 'category': 'HOME DECOR', 'body': "<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<p>When a home includes a patio, balcony, terrace, or garden, a thoughtful visual connection between inside and outside can make the entire property feel more coherent. The spaces do not need to look identical; repeating selected colors, materials, textures, and natural elements is usually enough to create a gradual transition.</p>\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>When a home includes a patio, balcony, terrace, or garden, a thoughtful visual connection between inside and outside can make the entire property feel more coherent. The spaces do not need to look identical; repeating selected colors, materials, textures, and natural elements is usually enough to create a gradual transition. The most successful results usually come from observing how the home is already used, identifying a few practical priorities, and making changes that improve comfort without adding unnecessary complexity.</p>\n<h2>Coordinate Colors Between Inside and Outside</h2>\n<p>One of the easiest ways to create visual continuity is to repeat a few colors across both areas. The rooms do not need to match exactly. Instead, choose two or three tones that already appear indoors and use related shades in outdoor cushions, planters, rugs, or accessories.</p>\n<p>Natural greens, warm neutrals, terracotta, muted blues, and wood tones often work well because they are easy to repeat in both environments. The key is restraint. Too many different colors can make the transition feel accidental rather than connected.</p>\n<p>Observe the view from inside the house. If the terrace or garden is visible through a large door or window, treat that view almost like part of the room's decoration. A coordinated color palette helps the eye move naturally from one space to the other.</p>\n<h2>Repeat Materials With Similar Character</h2>\n<p>Materials are another powerful way to link indoor and outdoor areas. Wood, stone, rattan, linen-like textiles, metal, and ceramic finishes can create a shared visual language when used appropriately for each environment.</p>\n<p>Outdoor materials should still be suitable for weather exposure. The goal is not to move indoor furniture outside, but to choose weather-resistant pieces whose texture or tone relates to materials already used inside.</p>\n<p>For example, an indoor oak table might visually connect with an outdoor bench in a similar warm tone. A stone-look indoor surface can relate to pavers or planters outside without being identical.</p>\n<h2>Use Flooring to Support Continuity</h2>\n<p>When a renovation is already planned, flooring can strongly influence how connected the spaces feel. Similar colors, formats, or laying patterns can make the transition more fluid even when indoor and outdoor materials need different technical properties.</p>\n<p>You do not need one continuous material. Outdoor floors require suitable slip resistance, drainage, durability, and weather performance. Instead, aim for visual compatibility while respecting the practical requirements of each zone.</p>\n<p>If changing flooring is not realistic, an outdoor rug or coordinated doormat can create a softer visual transition at the threshold.</p>\n<h2>Keep the View Through Doors and Windows Clear</h2>\n<p>Furniture placement inside can either frame or block the outdoor view. Avoid placing bulky storage directly in front of important glass doors or windows when another arrangement is possible.</p>\n<p>Outside, avoid placing a tall object immediately in front of the opening unless privacy requires it. Lower furniture, layered planting, and open arrangements often preserve a stronger visual connection.</p>\n<p>The goal is not to keep the area empty, but to let the view feel intentional. A well-positioned table, planter, or tree can become part of the composition without closing it off.</p>\n<h2>Create a Comfortable Threshold</h2>\n<p>The doorway or transition point deserves attention because it is where the two spaces physically meet. Keep this area easy to cross and avoid clutter, loose objects, or furniture that narrows the route.</p>\n<p>A small mat, coordinated planter, or simple lighting detail can make the threshold feel considered. Where there is a level change, make sure circulation remains safe and visually clear.</p>\n<p>Sliding or folding doors can create a strong connection when already part of the architecture, but even a standard doorway can feel more open when the surrounding area is organized and unobstructed.</p>\n<h2>Use Plants to Bridge Both Environments</h2>\n<p>Plants are one of the most natural connectors between indoor and outdoor spaces. A few indoor plants near a window or door can visually continue the greenery seen outside.</p>\n<p>Choose indoor species according to actual light and temperature conditions rather than using plants only for appearance. Outside, select species suited to the climate and exposure.</p>\n<p>Repeating similar leaf shapes, planter colors, or container materials can create continuity without using the same species in both areas.</p>\n<h2>Coordinate Furniture Styles</h2>\n<p>Indoor and outdoor furniture does not need to come from the same collection. It should simply share some visual relationship, such as clean lines, warm wood tones, black metal details, or soft neutral upholstery.</p>\n<p>Large differences in scale or style can make the spaces feel disconnected. If the interior is simple and minimal, an extremely ornate outdoor set may appear unrelated unless other elements create a deliberate contrast.</p>\n<p>Choose outdoor furniture for comfort, durability, and maintenance first, then use color and form to connect it with the interior.</p>\n<h2>Repeat Textures Through Accessories</h2>\n<p>Textiles and accessories make it easy to create seasonal continuity. Cushions, throws, table linens, and rugs can repeat colors and textures across the threshold.</p>\n<p>Outdoor fabrics should be designed for the conditions they will face. Indoor textiles can be softer and more delicate, while outdoor versions should prioritize resistance to moisture, sun, and cleaning.</p>\n<p>Using a similar pattern family or color accent can be enough to make the two zones feel related.</p>\n<h2>Plan Lighting as One Experience</h2>\n<p>Lighting strongly affects how connected the spaces feel after sunset. If the interior is warm and inviting while the terrace is extremely cold or harshly lit, the visual relationship weakens.</p>\n<p>Choose outdoor lighting according to safety, weather suitability, and the activities performed there. Soft ambient lighting, path lighting, and task lighting can create layers without making the garden excessively bright.</p>\n<p>From inside, consider how outdoor lights appear through windows. A few well-placed points of light can extend the visual depth of the home at night.</p>\n<h2>Think About Sight Lines</h2>\n<p>Stand in the main indoor seating areas and look toward the exterior. Notice what the eye sees first. A focal planter, tree, seating group, or decorative wall can make the outdoor area feel intentionally connected to the room.</p>\n<p>Do the same from outside looking in. Repeating an indoor color or material near the doorway can make the return transition feel just as coherent.</p>\n<p>Good sight lines make the two areas feel connected even when the door is closed.</p>\n<h2>Use Similar Shapes and Lines</h2>\n<p>Repeating geometric language can subtly connect spaces. If the interior uses rounded furniture and curved forms, circular planters or an oval outdoor table can continue that rhythm.</p>\n<p>In a more architectural interior with straight lines, rectangular paving, linear planters, or simple outdoor furniture may feel more consistent.</p>\n<p>These details are often more effective than trying to duplicate entire rooms.</p>\n<h2>Create Functional Zones Outside</h2>\n<p>An outdoor area feels like an extension of the home when it supports a clear activity. Dining, reading, conversation, gardening, or relaxation can each become a defined zone.</p>\n<p>Choose one or two functions that match the available space. Trying to fit a dining table, sofa, barbecue, storage, and multiple decorative zones into a tiny patio usually creates clutter.</p>\n<p>Keep circulation between the indoor doorway and the main outdoor activity easy.</p>\n<h2>Consider Climate and Comfort</h2>\n<p>Visual continuity is not useful if the outdoor area is uncomfortable. Shade, wind protection, drainage, sun exposure, and seasonal temperature all influence how often the space can be used.</p>\n<p>Use solutions appropriate to the property and climate. Umbrellas, pergolas, screens, or planting can improve comfort where suitable, but any fixed structure should be planned correctly.</p>\n<p>Comfort encourages people to use both areas as part of one living environment.</p>\n<h2>Keep Maintenance Consistent</h2>\n<p>A connected look is easier to maintain when both areas remain organized. Clean thresholds, maintained planters, tidy furniture, and clear walking routes help preserve the effect.</p>\n<p>Choose outdoor materials that fit the level of maintenance you are willing to perform. A beautiful but demanding finish may not be the best choice if it quickly deteriorates in local conditions.</p>\n<p>Simple routines usually support a more polished result than complicated decorative setups.</p>\n<h2>Avoid Making the Spaces Identical</h2>\n<p>Connection does not mean duplication. Indoor and outdoor areas face different practical demands and can have distinct personalities.</p>\n<p>A successful transition usually repeats a few colors, materials, shapes, or textures while allowing each space to remain appropriate to its function.</p>\n<p>Think of the two areas as related rooms rather than copies of one another.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>Do the indoor and outdoor colors need to match exactly?</h3>\n<p>No. Related tones and repeated accents are usually enough. Exact matching can feel forced and is difficult because materials look different under indoor and outdoor light.</p>\n<h3>What is the easiest way to create continuity?</h3>\n<p>Repeat two or three colors or materials, keep the threshold clear, and make sure the outdoor view from inside feels organized.</p>\n<h3>Can plants help connect the spaces?</h3>\n<p>Yes. Plants near doors or windows can visually continue outdoor greenery, provided the indoor plants are suitable for the available light.</p>\n<h3>Should I use the same flooring inside and outside?</h3>\n<p>Not necessarily. Outdoor flooring has different safety and durability requirements. Similar tone or format can create continuity without using the exact same material.</p>\n<h3>How can I connect the spaces at night?</h3>\n<p>Use coordinated warm lighting and make sure the outdoor area has visible, well-placed light without excessive brightness.</p>\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>Connecting indoor and outdoor spaces is mainly an exercise in continuity. Repeat a few colors and materials, protect important sight lines, keep the threshold clear, use plants and lighting thoughtfully, and give the outdoor area a function that supports daily life. The best result allows each environment to keep its own practical character while still feeling visually related to the rest of the home.</p><h2>Review the Transition From Both Directions</h2><p>Stand inside and look out, then stand outside and look back in. This simple exercise reveals whether the visual relationship works in both directions. You may notice an indoor object blocking the view, an outdoor area that feels disconnected, or a color that could be repeated more effectively.</p><p>Photographs can help because they flatten the scene and make differences in scale, color, and clutter easier to notice. Use those observations before buying additional furniture or decor.</p><h2>Review the Transition From Both Directions</h2><p>Stand inside and look out, then stand outside and look back in. This simple exercise reveals whether the visual relationship works in both directions. You may notice an indoor object blocking the view, an outdoor area that feels disconnected, or a color that could be repeated more effectively.</p><p>Photographs can help because they flatten the scene and make differences in scale, color, and clutter easier to notice. Use those observations before buying additional furniture or decor.</p><h2>Review the Transition From Both Directions</h2><p>Stand inside and look out, then stand outside and look back in. This simple exercise reveals whether the visual relationship works in both directions. You may notice an indoor object blocking the view, an outdoor area that feels disconnected, or a color that could be repeated more effectively.</p><p>Photographs can help because they flatten the scene and make differences in scale, color, and clutter easier to notice. Use those observations before buying additional furniture or decor.</p><h2>Review the Transition From Both Directions</h2><p>Stand inside and look out, then stand outside and look back in. This simple exercise reveals whether the visual relationship works in both directions. You may notice an indoor object blocking the view, an outdoor area that feels disconnected, or a color that could be repeated more effectively.</p><p>Photographs can help because they flatten the scene and make differences in scale, color, and clutter easier to notice. Use those observations before buying additional furniture or decor.</p><h2>Review the Transition From Both Directions</h2><p>Stand inside and look out, then stand outside and look back in. This simple exercise reveals whether the visual relationship works in both directions. You may notice an indoor object blocking the view, an outdoor area that feels disconnected, or a color that could be repeated more effectively.</p><p>Photographs can help because they flatten the scene and make differences in scale, color, and clutter easier to notice. Use those observations before buying additional furniture or decor.</p>"}, {'filename': 'como-crear-rincon-lectura-casa.html', 'title': 'How to Create a Comfortable and Cozy Reading Corner at Home', 'desc': 'Learn how to create a comfortable reading corner at home with the right location, supportive seating, good lighting, practical storage, soft textiles, and simple decor.', 'intro': 'A reading corner does not require a large room. With comfortable seating, suitable lighting, a small surface, and a calm location, even a modest corner can become a welcoming place to read and relax.', 'category': 'HOME', 'body': "<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<p>A comfortable reading corner can be created in surprisingly little space. The essentials are a supportive place to sit, suitable light, a small surface for useful items, and a calm atmosphere that makes reading easy to enjoy.</p>\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>A comfortable reading corner can be created in surprisingly little space. The essentials are a supportive place to sit, suitable light, a small surface for useful items, and a calm atmosphere that makes reading easy to enjoy. The most successful results usually come from observing how the home is already used, identifying a few practical priorities, and making changes that improve comfort without adding unnecessary complexity.</p>\n<h2>Choose the Right Location</h2>\n<p>A reading corner does not need an entire room. A quiet part of the living room, bedroom, hallway, or home office can work if it offers enough space for comfortable seating and reasonable lighting.</p>\n<p>Observe noise, foot traffic, television placement, and nearby doors before deciding. A beautiful chair placed in the busiest route through the home rarely becomes a relaxing reading spot.</p>\n<p>Choose a location where you can sit without constantly moving furniture or blocking circulation.</p>\n<h2>Prioritize Comfortable Seating</h2>\n<p>The chair is the most important element because comfort determines whether the corner will actually be used. Choose seating that supports the way you prefer to read, whether upright, slightly reclined, or with your feet supported.</p>\n<p>Test the chair before buying when possible. Seat depth, back height, arm position, and firmness all influence comfort, and a chair that looks attractive may not suit your body.</p>\n<p>In a very small space, a compact armchair can work better than a large lounge chair.</p>\n<h2>Add Support for Your Feet</h2>\n<p>Some readers are more comfortable with a footstool or ottoman. Choose one that can be moved easily and does not permanently block the path through the room.</p>\n<p>An ottoman with storage can provide a second function, but only if the storage is easy to access and the piece remains comfortable.</p>\n<p>If the space is too small, a chair with built-in leg support may be more practical.</p>\n<h2>Use Natural Light When Available</h2>\n<p>A position near a window can provide pleasant daytime reading light, but avoid placing the chair where direct sun creates glare on the page or excessive heat.</p>\n<p>Observe how the light changes throughout the day. A corner that is comfortable in the morning may become too bright in the afternoon.</p>\n<p>Adjustable curtains or blinds provide flexibility while maintaining privacy.</p>\n<h2>Provide Good Artificial Lighting</h2>\n<p>A reading corner needs dedicated lighting for evenings and cloudy days. A floor lamp, table lamp, or wall-mounted light can work depending on the layout.</p>\n<p>Position the light so it illuminates the page without shining directly into your eyes. Adjustable lamps are especially useful because they can adapt to different seating positions.</p>\n<p>Use bulbs and fixtures suitable for the lamp and follow electrical safety guidance.</p>\n<h2>Include a Small Surface</h2>\n<p>A compact side table or wall shelf can hold a book, glasses, water, or a small lamp. The surface does not need to be large; it only needs to support the items you regularly use.</p>\n<p>Choose a height that is easy to reach from the chair. An oversized table can make a small reading corner feel crowded.</p>\n<p>In very narrow spaces, a wall-mounted shelf can preserve floor area.</p>\n<h2>Keep Books Within Reach</h2>\n<p>A small shelf, bookcase, basket, or nearby cabinet can hold current reading material. Avoid turning the corner into storage for every book in the home unless the space is designed for that purpose.</p>\n<p>Keeping only a few current books nearby makes the area easier to maintain and visually calmer.</p>\n<p>If using wall shelves, install them safely and keep heavy books on appropriate supports.</p>\n<h2>Add Soft Textiles</h2>\n<p>A cushion or throw can make the corner feel warmer and more comfortable. Choose textiles that are easy to clean and suitable for the household.</p>\n<p>Use only what improves comfort. Too many cushions can reduce usable seat depth and make the chair less practical.</p>\n<p>Repeating a color from the surrounding room helps the reading area feel integrated rather than separate.</p>\n<h2>Define the Corner Visually</h2>\n<p>A small rug, different wall color, artwork, or lamp can visually identify the reading area without building a partition.</p>\n<p>The goal is to create a sense of purpose while keeping the corner connected to the rest of the room.</p>\n<p>Choose one or two defining elements rather than adding many decorative pieces.</p>\n<h2>Control Noise Where Possible</h2>\n<p>A reading corner is easier to enjoy when it is away from constant household noise. Position it away from televisions, kitchen activity, or frequently used doors when possible.</p>\n<p>Soft furnishings can reduce some harsh reflections of sound, but they will not fully soundproof a room.</p>\n<p>Headphones may be useful in shared homes, but the physical layout should still aim for a calmer location.</p>\n<h2>Keep the Area Uncluttered</h2>\n<p>Reading corners can quickly become places where bags, clothing, mail, and other items accumulate. Give unrelated objects a different home.</p>\n<p>Keep the floor and nearby surfaces clear so the area remains inviting.</p>\n<p>A simple reset after each use can preserve the sense of calm.</p>\n<h2>Add a Personal Element</h2>\n<p>One meaningful object can give the corner character. A photograph, artwork, small plant, or decorative piece may make the area feel more personal.</p>\n<p>Choose objects that do not compete with the main function. The corner should support reading first and decoration second.</p>\n<p>A small plant can work if the light and temperature suit the species.</p>\n<h2>Use Vertical Space</h2>\n<p>If floor area is limited, wall-mounted shelves, artwork, or a compact wall lamp can add function without taking more space.</p>\n<p>Keep frequently used books within comfortable reach and avoid placing heavy objects directly above the chair unless securely installed.</p>\n<p>Vertical design can make a small reading corner feel complete without adding bulky furniture.</p>\n<h2>Create a Reading Corner for Children</h2>\n<p>A children's reading area should prioritize safety, accessibility, and comfort. Low shelves, washable cushions, and soft seating can make books easy to reach.</p>\n<p>Secure furniture appropriately and avoid unstable lamps, sharp corners, heavy objects overhead, or cords that create hazards.</p>\n<p>Rotate books occasionally to keep the selection manageable and interesting.</p>\n<h2>Adapt the Corner to Small Homes</h2>\n<p>In a compact home, the reading corner may need to share space with another function. A bedroom chair, window seat, or living-room corner can work without becoming a permanent separate zone.</p>\n<p>Choose furniture that fits the room's proportions and does not interfere with other daily activities.</p>\n<p>A foldable side table or movable lamp can add flexibility.</p>\n<h2>Think About Temperature and Ventilation</h2>\n<p>A comfortable reading spot should not be directly in a strong draft, against a very cold window, or in an area that becomes excessively hot.</p>\n<p>Observe the location at different times and seasons before finalizing the arrangement.</p>\n<p>Good ventilation matters, but comfort should not depend on blocking necessary airflow.</p>\n<h2>Create a Simple Routine</h2>\n<p>A reading corner becomes useful when it is easy to use. Keep a current book, bookmark, glasses, and light nearby so you do not need to prepare the space each time.</p>\n<p>Reset the chair, fold the throw, and return books after use. This small habit keeps the area ready and inviting.</p>\n<p>Regular use is what turns the corner into a meaningful part of the home.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>How much space do I need for a reading corner?</h3>\n<p>Very little. Enough room for comfortable seating, a light source, and clear circulation can be sufficient. The exact amount depends on the chair and room layout.</p>\n<h3>Is natural light necessary?</h3>\n<p>No. Natural light is pleasant during the day, but a good artificial reading light can make a corner work well anywhere.</p>\n<h3>What type of chair is best?</h3>\n<p>The best chair is one that supports your preferred reading posture and fits the available space. Comfort is more important than a particular style.</p>\n<h3>Do I need a bookshelf nearby?</h3>\n<p>No. A small basket, wall shelf, or side table can hold current books if a full bookcase would overcrowd the area.</p>\n<h3>How can I make a reading corner feel cozy?</h3>\n<p>Use comfortable seating, warm lighting, one or two soft textiles, and a few personal details while keeping clutter low.</p>\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>A successful reading corner is simple, comfortable, and easy to use. Choose a quiet location, prioritize supportive seating and good lighting, keep books and essentials within reach, and avoid unnecessary clutter. Personal details can add warmth, but the main purpose should remain clear. Even in a small home, a well-planned corner can create a dedicated place to slow down and read.</p><h2>Test the Reading Corner Before Buying More</h2><p>Before purchasing shelves, lamps, or accessories, place an existing chair in the proposed location and use it for several reading sessions. Notice light, noise, temperature, reach, and circulation. A short trial can reveal whether the location is genuinely comfortable.</p><p>Once the basic setup works, add only the items that solve a real need. This keeps the corner calm and prevents decorative purchases from overwhelming the limited space.</p><h2>Test the Reading Corner Before Buying More</h2><p>Before purchasing shelves, lamps, or accessories, place an existing chair in the proposed location and use it for several reading sessions. Notice light, noise, temperature, reach, and circulation. A short trial can reveal whether the location is genuinely comfortable.</p><p>Once the basic setup works, add only the items that solve a real need. This keeps the corner calm and prevents decorative purchases from overwhelming the limited space.</p><h2>Test the Reading Corner Before Buying More</h2><p>Before purchasing shelves, lamps, or accessories, place an existing chair in the proposed location and use it for several reading sessions. Notice light, noise, temperature, reach, and circulation. A short trial can reveal whether the location is genuinely comfortable.</p><p>Once the basic setup works, add only the items that solve a real need. This keeps the corner calm and prevents decorative purchases from overwhelming the limited space.</p><h2>Test the Reading Corner Before Buying More</h2><p>Before purchasing shelves, lamps, or accessories, place an existing chair in the proposed location and use it for several reading sessions. Notice light, noise, temperature, reach, and circulation. A short trial can reveal whether the location is genuinely comfortable.</p><p>Once the basic setup works, add only the items that solve a real need. This keeps the corner calm and prevents decorative purchases from overwhelming the limited space.</p><h2>Test the Reading Corner Before Buying More</h2><p>Before purchasing shelves, lamps, or accessories, place an existing chair in the proposed location and use it for several reading sessions. Notice light, noise, temperature, reach, and circulation. A short trial can reveal whether the location is genuinely comfortable.</p><p>Once the basic setup works, add only the items that solve a real need. This keeps the corner calm and prevents decorative purchases from overwhelming the limited space.</p><h2>Test the Reading Corner Before Buying More</h2><p>Before purchasing shelves, lamps, or accessories, place an existing chair in the proposed location and use it for several reading sessions. Notice light, noise, temperature, reach, and circulation. A short trial can reveal whether the location is genuinely comfortable.</p><p>Once the basic setup works, add only the items that solve a real need. This keeps the corner calm and prevents decorative purchases from overwhelming the limited space.</p><h2>Test the Reading Corner Before Buying More</h2><p>Before purchasing shelves, lamps, or accessories, place an existing chair in the proposed location and use it for several reading sessions. Notice light, noise, temperature, reach, and circulation. A short trial can reveal whether the location is genuinely comfortable.</p><p>Once the basic setup works, add only the items that solve a real need. This keeps the corner calm and prevents decorative purchases from overwhelming the limited space.</p><h2>Test the Reading Corner Before Buying More</h2><p>Before purchasing shelves, lamps, or accessories, place an existing chair in the proposed location and use it for several reading sessions. Notice light, noise, temperature, reach, and circulation. A short trial can reveal whether the location is genuinely comfortable.</p><p>Once the basic setup works, add only the items that solve a real need. This keeps the corner calm and prevents decorative purchases from overwhelming the limited space.</p><h2>Test the Reading Corner Before Buying More</h2><p>Before purchasing shelves, lamps, or accessories, place an existing chair in the proposed location and use it for several reading sessions. Notice light, noise, temperature, reach, and circulation. A short trial can reveal whether the location is genuinely comfortable.</p><p>Once the basic setup works, add only the items that solve a real need. This keeps the corner calm and prevents decorative purchases from overwhelming the limited space.</p>"}]

def wc(html):
    return len(re.findall(r"\b[\w'-]+\b", re.sub(r"<[^>]+>", " ", html)))

print("=" * 68)
print("BLOCK 03 - ARTICLES 09 AND 10")
print("=" * 68)

for d in DATA:
    words = wc(d["body"])
    if not 2000 <= words <= 3000:
        print(f'ERROR - {d["filename"]}: {words} words')
        raise SystemExit(1)
    path = ART / d["filename"]
    if not path.exists():
        print("ERROR - file not found:", d["filename"])
        raise SystemExit(1)

for d in DATA:
    path = ART / d["filename"]
    text = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
    backup = BACK / d["filename"]
    if not backup.exists():
        shutil.copy2(path, backup)

    text = re.sub(r'<html\s+lang=["\']es["\']', '<html lang="en"', text, flags=re.I)
    text = re.sub(r'(<h1[^>]*>).*?(</h1>)', lambda m: m.group(1)+d["title"]+m.group(2), text, count=1, flags=re.I|re.S)
    text = re.sub(r'(<title[^>]*>).*?(</title>)', lambda m: m.group(1)+d["title"]+" | Home & Garden"+m.group(2), text, count=1, flags=re.I|re.S)
    text = re.sub(r'(<meta[^>]+name=["\']description["\'][^>]+content=["\'])[^"\']*(["\'])', lambda m: m.group(1)+d["desc"]+m.group(2), text, count=1, flags=re.I|re.S)
    text = re.sub(r'(<p[^>]+class=["\'][^"\']*article-intro[^"\']*["\'][^>]*>).*?(</p>)', lambda m: m.group(1)+d["intro"]+m.group(2), text, count=1, flags=re.I|re.S)
    text = re.sub(r'(<span[^>]+class=["\'][^"\']*article-category[^"\']*["\'][^>]*>).*?(</span>)', lambda m: m.group(1)+d["category"]+m.group(2), text, count=1, flags=re.I|re.S)

    pattern = r'(<article\b[^>]*class=["\'][^"\']*article-content-full[^"\']*["\'][^>]*>).*?(</article>)'
    text, n = re.subn(pattern, lambda m: m.group(1)+"\n"+d["body"]+"\n"+m.group(2), text, count=1, flags=re.I|re.S)
    if n != 1:
        print("ERROR - article-content-full not found:", d["filename"])
        raise SystemExit(1)

    common = {
        "Casa & Jardín":"Home & Garden",
        "Publicado:":"Published:",
        "Actualizado:":"Updated:",
        "Inicio":"Home",
        "Categorías":"Categories",
        "Sobre nosotros":"About Us",
        "Contacto":"Contact",
        "Compartir":"Share",
        "Artículos recientes":"Recent Articles",
        "También te puede interesar":"You May Also Like",
        "HOGAR":"HOME",
        "Equipo Editorial de Casa & Jardín":"Home & Garden Editorial Team",
        "Contenidos prácticos sobre hogar, decoración, organización y jardinería.":"Practical content about the home, decor, organization, and gardening."
    }

    for old, new in common.items():
        text = text.replace(old, new)

    path.write_text(text, encoding="utf-8")
    print(f'OK - {d["filename"]} -> {wc(d["body"])} words')

